\fs22\lang2057\langfe2057\loch\af31506\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af39 \ltrch\fcs0 \f39\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 Math Parser
\par \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 Math Parser is a simple library and interactive terminal for parsing mathematical expressions.
\par \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 When used in library mode, you create a Node object, Set it's expression, then calculate the result. You may create variables to store nodes and future expressions may refer to these variables.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af39\afs40 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f39\fs40\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 Expressions}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af39 \ltrch\fcs0 \f39\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 
\par \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 At its heart Math Parser is an expression calculat\hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 
or. It takes mathematical expressions, parses them and calculates the result. An expression is any string of numbers and mathematical operators. Expressions may also contain variables, which will be discussed in the next section.
\par \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 When used in interactive m\hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 ode, you can enter an expression at the terminal and when you hit enter the expression will be evaluated and the result displayed on the screen.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af40 \ltrch\fcs0 \f40\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 \hich\af40\dbch\af31505\loch\f40 math> 27/2+1\line 14.5
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af39 \ltrch\fcs0 \f39\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 Math Parser understands brackets, power, divide, multiply, add and subtract. Use the symbols \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 
( ) ^ / * + - for each operation. It applies the operators in the order above as per standard mathematical priority. Spaces and tabs are ignored and will be removed from the text before parsing. A newline ends the expression and causes it to be calculated
\hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39  \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 and the result is dislayed.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af39\afs40 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f39\fs40\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 Variables}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af39 \ltrch\fcs0 \f39\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 
\par \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39  You can assign an expression to a variable using the syntax
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af40 \ltrch\fcs0 \f40\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 \hich\af40\dbch\af31505\loch\f40 variable_name=expression}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af39 \ltrch\fcs0 \f39\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 
\par \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 You can then use the variable in further expressions. When you assign a variable, the result of the expression is not evaluated and \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 
displayed, it is only evaluated when needed.  For example, the following will calculate the area of a circle sector from its arc length and radius, calculating the sector angle as it does so.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af40 \ltrch\fcs0 \f40\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 \hich\af40\dbch\af31505\loch\f40 math> radius=5\line math> arcLength=4\line math> angle=arcLength/radius\line ma\hich\af40\dbch\af31505\loch\f40 th> radius^2*angle/2\line 10

\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af39 \ltrch\fcs0 \f39\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 
In the example above, the radius, arcLength and angle variables are not evaluated until the final line where the area is calculated and displayed.
\par \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 It is important to note that you are saving the expression to the variable, not the r\hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 
esult of evaluating the expression. This is different to many programmig languages. This means that if you change a variable, you will change the evaluation of any dependent variables. Continuing the above example:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af40 \ltrch\fcs0 \f40\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 \hich\af40\dbch\af31505\loch\f40 math> angle\line 0.8\line math> arcLength=2\line math> a\hich\af40\dbch\af31505\loch\f40 ngle\line 0.4
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af39 \ltrch\fcs0 \f39\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 You can see that when you change the arcLength variable, the angle variable changes as well.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af40 \ltrch\fcs0 \f40\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 \hich\af40\dbch\af31505\loch\f40 math> arcLength=2\line math> arclength}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af39 \ltrch\fcs0 \f39\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid11303026 
\par \hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 This property means you should be careful of recursive assignments. The following will cause the parser to enter an infi\hich\af39\dbch\af31505\loch\f39 nite loop and crash
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af39 \ltrch\fcs0 \f41\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid2294290\charrsid2294290 \hich\af41\dbch\af31505\loch\f41 math> x = x+1
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid2294290 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid2294290 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
The above examples show use of scalar variables, however, each variable is actually stored in a 2 dimensional array. In the above cases, the variables just have a size of 1 in both dimensions.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid10054484 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506  You can create a 1 or 2 dimen\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 sional variable like this}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid2294290 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f41\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid10054484\charrsid10054484 \hich\af41\dbch\af31505\loch\f41 math> x = \{1,2\}\line math> y = \{\{1,2\}\{3,4\}\}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f41\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid1918004\charrsid10054484 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid10054484 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid10054484 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
Use curly brackets to enclose the array and rows within a 2 dimensional array and use commas to separate values on each row.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid1918004 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 You can also create an\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506  empty variable like this
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f41\lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid1918004\charrsid10054484 \hich\af41\dbch\af31505\loch\f41 math> x =}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid1918004 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang9\langfe2057\langnp9\insrsid10054484 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 When you do maths on variables of different sizes, then the maximu\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
